{"name":"Horizon","hex":"#648894","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"horizon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible horizontal line (in all directions) where the sky appears to meet the earth in the distance.","The range or limit of one's knowledge, experience or interest; a boundary or threshold."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/horizon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Horizon","blocks":[{"text":"Most commonly, the horizon is the border between the surface of a celestial body and its sky when viewed from the perspective of an observer on or above the surface of the celestial body. This concept is further refined as:"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Horizons.svg/330px-Horizons.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizon","wikidata":"Q43261"},{"title":"Horizon (video game series)","blocks":[{"text":"Horizon is a science fiction franchise set in a 31st-century post-apocalyptic version of the United States. It primarily consists of action role-playing games. The franchise consists of two main games, Horizon Zero Dawn (2017) and Horizon Forbidden West (2022). The series also includes two spin-offs; Horizon Call of the Mountain (2023), a virtual reality game, and Lego Horizon Adventures (2024), a family-friendly retelling of Zero Dawn. The franchise also includes adaptations into comic books, Lego sets and tabletop games. Upcoming entries in the series include Horizon Steel Frontiers, an MMORPG, and Horizon Hunters Gathering, a cooperative multiplayer game, alongside a feature film adaptation of Zero Dawn currently in development. The narrative follows Aloy, a young Nora tribe huntress and a clone of 21st-century scientist Elisabet Sobeck. She attempts to uncover the secrets of the ancient past and stop recurring extinction-level threats."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizon_(video_game_series)","wikidata":"Q96316244"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/500px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/1280px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/500px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The curvature of Earth's horizon as observed from a Space Shuttle at an altitude of 226 km (140 mi).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth's_horizon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg/500px-Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg/1280px-Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg/500px-Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bird's-eye view created with a horizon line above the subject of the scene (in this case, Paris in 1850).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParis_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Rising_over_the_horizon.jpg/500px-Rising_over_the_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Rising_over_the_horizon.jpg/500px-Rising_over_the_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A sequence of pictures taken at Strait of Magellan, Chile, showing a tanker ship sailing towards the camera and rising over the horizon, demonstrating the Earth's curvature. Distance at first picture is about 18 km which would imply a theoretical horizon vertical drop of about 23 meters. Ship length is 186 m.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARising_over_the_horizon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/GeometricDistanceToHorizon.png/500px-GeometricDistanceToHorizon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/GeometricDistanceToHorizon.png/500px-GeometricDistanceToHorizon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Geometrical distance to the horizon, Pythagorean theorem","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeometricDistanceToHorizon.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/HomogSphElevObsZmax.png/500px-HomogSphElevObsZmax.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/HomogSphElevObsZmax.png/500px-Ho